<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awadh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oudh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awadh, or Oudh, is a region of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the Himalayan foothills. From the 1720s it was ruled by the Nawabs of Awadh, first as Mughal governors and then as near-independent rulers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>. The British annexed it in 1856, a grievance that fed the rebellion of 1857, and joined it to the United Provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awadh.jpg" alt="Awadh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: रूही · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarayu_River_night_view,_Ayodhya_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Awadh, known in British texts as Oudh, is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> that now forms the central part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. Its name comes from the Sanskrit Ayodhya, meaning not to be warred against, and it is roughly synonymous with the ancient Kosala of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, Buddhist and Jain scriptures. Known as the granary of India, Awadh mattered strategically for control of the Doab, the fertile plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> made the region part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> around 1555, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> it was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">subahs</a> into which he divided the empire in 1590. Saadat Khan, a Persian adventurer, was appointed its governor in 1722 and laid the foundation of the Awadh dynasty as Mughal power weakened. The Nawabs were a Persian Shia Muslim dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, and Persian was used in government and at court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a> fell out with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> after aiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a>.</p><p>The British appointed a resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> in 1773, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-ud-daula">Asaf-ud-Daula</a> moved the capital there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> in 1775. A treaty of 1801 with Saadat Ali Khan ceded half of Awadh to the Company and left the nawabs ceremonial kings, though the state remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in name until 1819. In that year Ghaziuddin Haidar began striking coins in his own name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1856 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> invaded and annexed the state under the Doctrine of Lapse. The Nawab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wajid-ali-shah">Wajid Ali Shah</a>, was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. In the rebellion of 1857 his 14-year-old son Birjis Qadra was crowned ruler, and the rebels held the region until the British reconquered it after 18 months, including the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>. In 1902 the new name United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> and Oudh was introduced.</p><p>The nawabs are remembered as patrons of arts, music and architecture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-imambara">Bara Imambara</a>, the Chhota Imambara and the Rumi Darwaza survive at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, and the nawabs left a composite culture known as the Ganga-Jamuni Tehzeeb. Ayodhya, in the region, is one of the seven holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Awadh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghazi-ud-Din Haidar Shah instituted the <b>Oudh</b> Bequest, fixed payments by the British to the Shia holy cities of Najaf and Karbala, paid as interest on a loan Oudh made to the Company in 1825.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On becoming Governor-General in 1772, Hastings rapidly expanded the Presidency's army, recruiting high-caste Rajputs and Brahmins of eastern <b>Awadh</b> and Bihar, the Purbiyas, who came to make up up to eighty per cent of the Bengal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Aurangzeb’s death in 1707, a rapid succession of weak rulers failed to hold the empire together, and provincial governors in Hyderabad, Bengal and <b>Awadh</b> established effectively autonomous states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Conversion lightened the tax under Firuz Shah, while a village that resisted or failed to pay could be enslaved: Balban had ordered it in <b>Awadh</b>, and Alauddin Khalji made it lawful for revenue defaulters.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maratha Empire defeated the combined army of the Nizam of Hyderabad and several Mughal generals at Bhopal, a coalition that also took in the Rajputs of Jaipur and the Nawabs of <b>Awadh</b> and Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1722 the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah appointed Saadat Ali Khan, a Shia noble from Nishapur, as governor of <b>Awadh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/awadh-state">Awadh State</a></li></ul></section>
